Q: Best approach for generating PDF with different Blade templates and deleting after use? A: <p>Your flow is correct: DB key, Blade in the code, queue job, render, send, delete. Change three things in it.</p>
<h2>1. Two jobs can write to the same file</h2>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$path = storage_path('pdf_bills/' . $bill-&gt;hr_ref . '.pdf');
</code></pre>
<p>The file name comes from the bill. Each run for the bill <code>ABC123</code> writes to the same path. This causes a failure when two workers process one bill at the same time. Queue retries make this condition normal, not rare.</p>
<ol>
<li>The job starts. It writes the PDF. It sends the mail.</li>
<li>The mail server is slow. Laravel decides that the job failed.</li>
<li>Laravel starts a retry. The retry writes to the same path.</li>
<li>The first job sends an incomplete PDF.</li>
</ol>
<p>Both attempts report success. A customer gives you the first report of the failure.</p>
<p>Give the temporary file a random name. Then two runs cannot select the same name.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$path = sprintf('%s/bill-%s.pdf', sys_get_temp_dir(), Str::uuid());
</code></pre>
<p>The customer must see the bill reference. But that name applies to the attachment, not to the file on the disk.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>-&gt;attach($path, ['as' =&gt; $bill-&gt;hr_ref . '.pdf'])
</code></pre>
<p>Add <code>ShouldBeUnique</code> to the job. If you send the same bill two times, Laravel makes only one queue entry.</p>
<h2>2. The cleanup does not run when a failure occurs</h2>
<p>You delete the PDF only after a successful send. Therefore the delete does not occur after a failure.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$pdf-&gt;save($path);
Mail::to($user)-&gt;send(new BillMail($path));
unlink($path); // this line does not run if the send fails
</code></pre>
<p>Each failed send leaves a file on the disk. The files collect until the disk becomes full.</p>
<p>Put the cleanup in a <code>finally</code> block. Then it runs after a success and after a failure.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>try {
    $pdf-&gt;save($path);
    Mail::to($user)-&gt;send(new BillMail($path));
} finally {
    if (is_file($path)) {
        @unlink($path);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>A <code>finally</code> block does not help if the system stops the worker. This occurs when the worker has too little memory. It also occurs when a timeout stops the render.</p>
<p>Add a scheduled command as a backup. Make the command delete each <code>bill-*.pdf</code> file that is more than one hour old.</p>
<p>This is important on Lambda and on similar systems. The <code>/tmp</code> directory has a limit of 512 MB. The system keeps the directory when it starts the container again. Files from one job stay there for the next job. When the directory becomes full, an unrelated job fails with the message <code>No space left on device</code>.</p>
<h2>3. Half of the templates are copies</h2>
<pre><code>electricity.blade.php
electricity-with-vat.blade.php
</code></pre>
<p>The two files are the same, but one file has a VAT section. The files for gas and water have the same condition. Therefore VAT doubles the number of templates. You must make each layout correction two times. This is the maintenance problem in your question.</p>
<p>Keep one template for each utility. Make VAT a conditional block.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>{{-- bill-templates/electricity.blade.php --}}
@include('bill-templates.partials.line-items', ['bill' =&gt; $bill])

@includeWhen($bill-&gt;vat_applicable, 'bill-templates.partials.vat-summary', ['bill' =&gt; $bill])
</code></pre>
<p>You now add one new file for each new utility. You make a VAT change in one location only.</p>
<p>One more point. The call <code>view('bill-templates.' . $bill-&gt;template)</code> renders the view that the column specifies. A user or an import can set this value. Use an enum for the column. Then a bad value causes an immediate failure.</p>
<h2>Two dompdf conditions</h2>
<p><strong>Memory, not the CPU, limits the number of bills that you render at the same time.</strong> dompdf keeps the full document in memory. A long multi-utility bill needs much more memory than a one-page electricity bill. Set the number of workers for the largest bill, not for the normal bill.</p>
<p><strong>dompdf 2.x does not support flexbox or grid.</strong> It uses floats and tables for the layout. Do not correct the bill markup during the Laravel upgrade. The result is different, and the cause is difficult to find.</p>
<h2>What to keep</h2>
<p>Keep the Blade templates in the code. Keep only the key in the database. If you put the template source in the database, the database content becomes code. You also lose the code review of the template changes.</p>

Q: Alternate positive and negative numbers in an array A: <p>PFB my code. If we will use Stack then it will make our problem more easier.</p>

<pre><code>public class AlternatePosNeg {
public static void main(String[] args) {
    int arr[] = { 2, -1, -3, -7, -8, 9, 5, -5, -7 };

    Stack&lt;Integer&gt; pos = new Stack&lt;&gt;();
    Stack&lt;Integer&gt; neg = new Stack&lt;&gt;();

    int i;

    for (i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] &gt; 0) {
            pos.push(arr[i]);
        } else {
            neg.push(arr[i]);
        }
    }

    int tempArr[] = new int[arr.length];

    i = 0;

    int sizePos = pos.size();
    int sizeNeg = neg.size();

    while (i &lt; tempArr.length) {
        if (sizePos &gt; sizeNeg) {
            if (pos.size() &gt; 0) {
                tempArr[i] = pos.pop();
            }
            if (neg.size() &gt; 0) {
                tempArr[i + 1] = neg.pop();
                i++;
            }
        } else {
            if (neg.size() &gt; 0) {
                tempArr[i] = neg.pop();
            }
            if (pos.size() &gt; 0) {
                tempArr[i + 1] = pos.pop();
                i++;
            }
        }

        i++;
    }

    for (int no : tempArr) {
        System.out.print(no + " ");
    }
}
</code></pre>

